Stretching across the windswept borders of western Uzbekistan and Kazakhstan lies one of the most staggering anthropogenic scars on the face of the Earth: **the Aralkum Desert, the desiccated corpse of the Aral Sea**.

As recently as 1960, the Aral was the **fourth-largest inland body of water on the planet**, spanning over 68,000 square kilometers (26,000 square miles)—larger than Switzerland or Ireland. It was a thriving, brackish aquatic inland empire teeming with sturgeon, carp, and a fishing fleet that supplied one-sixth of the entire Soviet Union's fish harvest.

Today, more than ninety percent of that water is gone, vaporized by catastrophic Soviet hydrological engineering that diverted the mighty Amu Darya and Syr Darya rivers to feed monoculture cotton plantations in the desert. 

What remains is an apocalyptic dust basin littered with the oxidising skeletons of massive commercial fishing trawlers stranded fifty miles from the nearest shoreline. 

Yet beneath the rusting iron of the ship cemeteries lies an even darker geopolitical secret: an isolated landmass once surrounded by open water—**Vozrozhdeniya Island**—which housed **Aralsk-7**, the Soviet Union’s top-secret proving ground for aerosolized anthrax, plague, and smallpox bioweapons.

