## The Vanishing Sea: Soviet "White Gold" and Ecocide

The death of the Aral Sea was not a natural climatic event; it was a calculated central-planning decision:
* **The Cotton Empire:** In the early 1960s, Soviet economic planners launched the "Virgin Lands" agricultural campaign, transforming the arid deserts of Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan into global hubs for raw cotton ("White Gold").
* **The Diversion Canals:** Engineers carved thousands of miles of unlined, leaky canals (including the gargantuan Karakum Canal) to siphon off the river waters feeding the Aral basin. As inflow plummeted from fifty-five billion cubic meters annually to virtually zero, the sea began its dramatic retreat.
* **The Aralkum Birth:** Within four decades, the shoreline receded by up to one hundred kilometers. The desiccated seabed transformed into a toxic, salt-encrusted wasteland known as the **Aralkum Desert**. Violent seasonal sandstorms now blow millions of tons of salt, pesticide residues (DDT), and carcinogenic agricultural chemical runoff across Central Asia, causing soaring rates of throat cancer, infant mortality, and respiratory disease in local Karakalpakstan populations.

